\title{ofeIntegrateR: Design, Sample and Analyse On-Farm Strip Experiments}
\description{
A workflow for on-farm experimentation (OFE) strip trials that runs
end to end without a commercial licence.
}
\section{Design the trial}{

\code{\link[=make_trial_design]{make_trial_design()}} lays out treatment plots in replicate blocks, as a
strip layout or two stacked tiers with a buffer, with randomised or
systematic treatment order.
}

\section{Plan the sampling}{

\code{\link[=kriging_sample_interval]{kriging_sample_interval()}} turns a variogram and a target precision into a
sampling interval and a core count -- and reports the precision floor the
nugget imposes. \code{\link[=place_point_samples]{place_point_samples()}} chooses the locations.
}

\section{Look at it}{

\code{\link[=ofe_map]{ofe_map()}} draws any column over the trial -- yield, a kriged soil surface,
elevation, the zones, the residuals -- and \code{plot()} methods cover the trial
design (\code{\link[=plot.ofe_design]{plot.ofe_design()}}), the zones (\code{\link[=plot.ofe_zones]{plot.ofe_zones()}}), the variogram
(\code{\link[=plot.ofe_variogram]{plot.ofe_variogram()}}) and a fitted model's diagnostics
(\code{\link[=plot.ofe_fit]{plot.ofe_fit()}}). \code{\link[=ofe_palette]{ofe_palette()}} holds the colours, chosen against a
colour-vision validator rather than by eye.
}

\section{Prepare the data}{

\code{\link[=grid_dense_layer]{grid_dense_layer()}} snaps an irregular yield-monitor cloud onto the
complete lattice a separable residual needs. \code{\link[=krige_point_samples]{krige_point_samples()}}
interpolates the sparse layer onto the same grid, and \code{\link[=cv_krige_surface]{cv_krige_surface()}}
says whether it was dense enough to be worth it. \code{\link[=ofe_variogram]{ofe_variogram()}} fits and
draws the variogram those steps depend on: the range says how far one core
speaks for, and the nugget says how much variation no sampling density will
ever resolve.
}

\section{Analyse}{

\code{\link[=fit_ofe]{fit_ofe()}} fits a spatial mixed model by REML with separable residual
structures -- \code{ar1()}, \code{id()}, \code{exp()}, \code{diag()}, and \code{dsum()} sections --
in base R. \code{\link[=wald_tests]{wald_tests()}} tests each fixed term as a whole and \code{\link[=ofe_means]{ofe_means()}}
gives predicted means and contrasts. The residual spellings follow ASReml-R,
and on the structures both support the estimates agree to several
significant figures, so a script written against either runs on the other.
}

\section{Report the means}{

\code{\link[=ofe_lsd]{ofe_lsd()}} gives the predicted means with their least significant
difference and the a/b/c letters, matching \code{agricolae::LSD.test()} and
\code{HSD.test()} on a balanced design. \code{\link[=compact_letters]{compact_letters()}} letters pairwise
comparisons from any other source.
}

\section{Pseudo-environments}{

Two ways to find them, for two different questions.
\code{\link[=partition_pseudo_env]{partition_pseudo_env()}} slices a strip trial across its length from the
pattern in the dense layer, using the fitted correlation range to stop the
partition running past the scale the data can support; every treatment stays
in every zone. \code{\link[=partition_paddock]{partition_paddock()}} zones a paddock on environmental
covariates -- elevation, EM38, soil tests -- into rectangular blocks by
default, so the zones are something a machine can drive and a sampling grid
can follow, with contiguous free-form zones available where the soil matters
more than the shape. \code{\link[=adaptive_residual]{adaptive_residual()}} writes the matching \code{dsum()}
residual formula for either, demoting \code{ar1()} to \code{id()} in any zone that
lacks the extent to support it.
}

\section{Integrate a point-source layer}{

Two strategies, each with an open-source path:

\describe{
\item{Kriged-covariate (\code{\link[=fit_integrated_kriged]{fit_integrated_kriged()}})}{Krige the sparse
variable onto the trial grid, then include the surface as a fixed
covariate in a spatial mixed model of the dense response. Pass
\code{covariate = NULL} for the dense-layer-only baseline the integrated fit
has to beat.}
\item{Joint bivariate model (\code{\link[=fit_integrated_joint]{fit_integrated_joint()}})}{Fit the dense
response and the point-source measurements jointly as two stacked
layers sharing a spatial random effect, with no separate kriging step.}
}

\code{\link[=compare_integration]{compare_integration()}} fits the baseline and the integrated model together,
and \code{\link[=extract_fixed_effects]{extract_fixed_effects()}} reads contrasts out of any of the fits.
}

\section{Simulate}{

\code{\link[=simulate_paddock]{simulate_paddock()}} builds a paddock whose truth is known: a correlated
yield-potential surface, covariate layers related to it by a chosen
correlation, pseudo-environments with their own treatment response, and a
trial laid into it -- so the zoning and the analysis can be scored rather
than merely run. \code{\link[=simulate_ofe_trial]{simulate_ofe_trial()}} gives a tidy lattice for quick
tests, and \code{\link[=simulate_yield_monitor]{simulate_yield_monitor()}} the awkward shape a real harvester
produces.

Developed for the AAGI-CU-RD-OFE GRDC project
(\dQuote{Development of processes to integrate point-source data and
high-resolution data}).
}

